<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Denkyira</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Denkyira was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state in what is now southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana">Ghana</a> that dominated the region in the seventeenth century, holding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> as tributaries. Its defeat by the Asante under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-feyiase">Battle of Feyiase</a> in 1701 marked the emergence of the Ashanti Empire as the leading power of the region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/denkyira.jpg" alt="Denkyira" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denkyrian_Empire_1700ce.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Oral traditions trace Denkyira's ancestors to the Bono area of the forest-savanna borderland, from which they moved south into Adanse, the older state to which early Denkyira was subordinate. The Agona, who founded the kingdom, lived on the western frontier of Adanse, and near the Oda and Ofin rivers they built a new capital, Abankeseso, also remembered as Ntibanso. European accounts of the 16th and early 17th centuries did not name Denkyira, grouping the inland gold traders of the forest as the Akani. After conquering Adanse, Denkyira became by the mid-17th century the dominant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> power of the Ofin-Pra basin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the later 17th century it defeated the Sefwi, Wassa and Aowin, taking their gold mines and the western routes to the coastal forts, and its victory over King Agyensam of Assin at Koshea secured its roads to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-coast">Cape Coast</a>. Around 1680 Abankeseso became what the historian Kea calls an imperial capital, ruling many subordinate towns. In 1692 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> envoys travelled inland to meet the Denkyirahene Boamponsem, who kept a resident envoy on the coast to collect tribute and regulate trade. Costly wars in the 1690s against Asen and Twifo-Heman drained the state, and after Boamponsem's death in 1694 his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ntim-gyakari">Ntim Gyakari</a>'s heavy tribute demands drove subject states towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osei-tutu-i">Osei Tutu</a> of Kwaaman.</p><p>At the Battle of Feyiase Ntim Gyakari was killed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> sacked Abankeseso and seized the gold of its Sikadan treasury. A revolt in 1706 and 1707 was crushed. After a later rebellion against Asante overlordship failed in the 19th century, the Denkyira left the Ofin-Pra basin for the south, settling at Jukwaa and Dunkwa-on-Offin, and in 1868 they joined the Fante Confederacy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kingdom was divided among wing chiefs, the nsafohene, who led their divisions in war and governed in peace: the Right Wing, the Left Wing and the Advance Guard. It took part in the shift from spears to firearms, conscripting all able-bodied men. Its merchants traded gold for textiles, iron bars, brassware and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> at English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> factories. Its regalia, among them the Abankamdwa stool and the Sasatia knife, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Asante</a> treasury at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumasi">Kumasi</a>. A non-sovereign Denkyira monarchy continues at Dunkwa-on-Offin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Denkyira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the time the Asante states of the interior were subject to <b>Denkyira</b>, a powerful forest kingdom to the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The leaders of Nsuta, Mampong, Dwaben, Bekwai and Kokofu had met before the final break with <b>Denkyira</b>, and after the victory at Feyiase in 1701 the allied rulers recognised Kumasi as the centre of the union.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>It was for a time subject to Denkyira, and after the collapse of <b>Denkyira</b> in 1701 it passed under the influence of the Ashanti Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/wassa">Wassa</a></li></ul></section>
